    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The full suite passes \u2014 **60 tests run, 0 failures, 0 errors, BUILD SUCCESS** \u2014 including the fiscal-year tests (Cp14, Cp48) and all the membership-points/level tests (Cp15, Cp23, Cp24, Cp39, Cp40) and the audit test (Cp43).\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe task moves three date-derived values to a fiscal year that starts on **1 July**, named `FY<YY>` where `YY` is the last two digits of the calendar year in which the fiscal year began (so a date in July\u2013Dec 2026 \u2192 `FY26`, Jan\u2013Jun 2026 \u2192 `FY25`).\n\n- **New `model/FiscalYears.java`** \u2014 a shared static helper (mirroring the existing `MembershipLevels` style) with `startYear(date)`, `label(date)` \u2192 `\"FY26\"`, and `elapsed(from, to)` \u2192 whole fiscal years crossed. Centralizing this keeps the membership number, fiscal year, and tenure rules from diverging.\n\n- **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 added a `fiscalYear` mapping (`FiscalYears.label` of the business-day-adjusted `registrationDate`), and changed the `membershipNumber` `-M<YY>` segment to use `FiscalYears.startYear(...) % 100` instead of the calendar year. Both derive from the same start year, so `membershipNumber` always agrees with `fiscalYear` (Cp14).\n\n- **`MembershipLevels.java`** \u2014 tenure now counts elapsed fiscal years (`FiscalYears.elapsed`) rather than a 365-day window; the +3 tenure points apply when at least one fiscal year has elapsed. A newly created owner still has zero tenure, preserving the existing points/level behaviour.\n\n- **`AuditOwnerCreated.java`** \u2014 the audit line's `membershipNumber` now uses the same fiscal-year segment, keeping it consistent with the response DTO (Cp43).\n\n- **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the read-only `fiscalYear` field and updated the `membershipNumber` and `membershipPoints` descriptions to reflect the fiscal-year basis (this also regenerates `OwnerDto` with the new field).\n\nOne judgement call worth flagging: the spec didn't pin down which calendar year names the fiscal year, so I used the convention \"named by the year it starts in\" \u2014 this makes a post-July-2026 registration `FY26`, matching the prior calendar-year behaviour and the `-M26` examples already in the spec. The tests only assert internal consistency and the `FY\\d{2}` format, both of which hold.",
